{
  "method_step": 3,
  "declared_at": "2026-11-18T01:16:37.598635-06:00",
  "why_this_direction": "Quantify whether fitted repair and limited cross-problem transfer followed the same recurring whole-path difference at the same installed checkpoints.",
  "repeating_pattern_check": "One-site separation and fitted scalar loss were already measured. Use every recorded transformation and two contemporaneous fitted/unfitted checkpoint comparisons. Omit both recipients and the training reference from the group means.",
  "hypothesis": "An update that predominantly follows a feature of the selected pair can leave the direction shared by other correct executions weakly engaged, especially on the unfitted problem.",
  "prediction": "At matched checkpoints, the fitted trajectory will move farther along the correct-minus-wrong donor contrast and closer to the frozen correct donor mean than the unfitted trajectory, relative to each recipient\u2019s own original path.",
  "would_weaken_it": "Comparable or larger motion by the unfitted recipient toward that contrast and toward the frozen correct mean despite its known failure.",
  "what_went_wrong_before": "An overall loss reduction could hide opposite movement in a specific computation. A group gap could shrink because both groups moved.",
  "rule": "Use full stored computation-only dynamic-time-warping correspondence to the original axis. Average the matched transformation vectors per member at each registered event and merge. Give each donor one vote. Let d=C\u2212W be the fixed correct-minus-wrong donor contrast and u=edited\u2212original the recipient movement. Report sum(u\u00b7d)/sum(d\u00b7d), cosine, and RMS distance to the fixed C before/after. Primary scope is the whole trace. Secondary scope uses all axis places where sum of squared donor gaps across merges exceeds twice summed donor variance of the estimated gap, as in the prior registered overlap. No hand-picked sites, code paths inferred from text, or externally supplied labels in registration. The existing cohort labels identify which group performed descent correctly. This readout measures group-average geometry, not an exhaustive model of successful computation.",
